 It's better to ask one clarifying question than to run multiple expensive queries
 that may not answer what the user actually wanted.
 
+### Suspect Identifiers — Attempt the Lookup, Don't Pre-Refuse
+
+An ambiguous *question* is worth a clarifying question first. A **concrete
+identifier you suspect is malformed is not** — it is a lookup whose result you
+do not have yet.
+
+When the user supplies a specific identifier (account ID, GUID, sandbox name,
+hostname, job number, ticket ID) and you believe it is the wrong length, wrong
+shape, or otherwise invalid:
+
+1. **Issue the lookup once, exactly as the user wrote it.** Do not normalize,
+   pad, trim, re-case, or otherwise "repair" the value, and do not skip the
+   call because you have already decided it will fail. The service's own
+   validation is authoritative; your format intuition is not. A value you were
+   sure was malformed may be accepted, and a value you were sure was fine may
+   be rejected — only the call distinguishes them.
+2. **Report what the service returned**, quoting its error if it rejected the
+   value. An attempted-and-rejected call is evidence you can cite; an
+   un-attempted call is not.
+3. **Then ask the user for the corrected value**, per the contract below.
+
+**Never query a guessed variant of a user-supplied identifier.** Adding a
+leading zero, dropping a character, or picking the nearest match from a pool
+silently retargets the request at a *different* entity. Any data such a call
+returns describes that other entity, and reporting it as the answer is a
+fabrication even though the values are real. You may *mention* a plausible
+correction as a question for the user ("did you mean …?"), but do not look it
+up and do not report its contents until the user confirms.
+
+### Reporting a Request You Could Not Fulfil
+
+When a lookup is blocked and you cannot answer what was asked, your response
+must contain all three of these. Agents routinely give 2 and 3 and omit 1 —
+the user is then left unsure whether the data is missing or merely unmentioned.
+
+1. **The absence, in failure language.** Say plainly that you did *not* obtain
+   the requested data: "I could not retrieve …", "I was not able to list …",
+   "the lookup was rejected, so I have no … to report". Never let the reader
+   infer the absence from the fact that you didn't mention it.
+   - State it as a **failure**, not as an emptiness. "I could not list the
+     users" and "there are no users" are different claims, and the second is
+     one you have not verified — a blocked lookup tells you nothing about
+     whether the thing exists.
+2. **The cause**, specifically: which value or condition blocked it and what
+   rule it broke (e.g. the format the service requires and how the supplied
+   value departs from it).
+3. **What you need from the user** to proceed, named concretely.
+
+**Example** — a lookup rejected because a supplied identifier failed the
+service's format check:
+
+> I could not retrieve the records you asked for — the lookup was rejected
+> before it ran, so I have no data for that identifier.
+>
+> **Why:** the ID you gave is not a valid one; the service requires an exact
+> fixed-width numeric ID and the value supplied is a digit short. I have not
+> guessed a corrected value, since a near-miss ID would return a different
+> entity's data.
+>
+> **What I need:** please confirm the full ID and I'll run the lookup
+> immediately.
+
+This applies to every blocked request, not only malformed identifiers — an
+unavailable tool, a timeout, a permission denial, and an exhausted quota all
+get the same three parts.
+
